# A complete, winning age-1 arc from the shipped opening (Step060 test).
#
# The opening as shipped is blocked: two active workshops emit +2 pollution
# per tick with no park to absorb it, so quality hits 0, the admission gate
# closes and population stays pinned at 10/10 (measured: critical at t=120
# and t=300 in scenarios/opening.jsonl).
#
# The fix, in the order the arithmetic demands:
#   1. cropRotation (age 1, 50$) makes a farm yield 3/tick. That is what
#      breaks the age-1 infeasibility — with one worker per building slot,
#      f + w + 2 workshops = P cannot satisfy both 2f+1 >= P and 2w >= P.
#      At 3/tick, six farms feed 16 colonists AND keep a reserve (Step068):
#      five farms produce exactly the consumption (16), which has no fixed
#      point once conservation takes 3%/tick — the colony then starves on the
#      boundary, one death and one admission per tick (measured).
#   2. One park absorbs 2/tick against the workshops' 2/tick: net 0, quality
#      100. The second park of the Step060 line was pure upkeep (Step068), and
#      the one that is left now ships with the opening (D2) — see the note
#      below, where the line used to build it.
#   3. Capacity is bought in the order that gates admission: water first,
#      then housing. Money is the tight constraint (~232$ on hand), so one
#      road cell is shared by two wells and the last two residences wait for
#      the tax of the first four to land.
# End state (Step068, measured): pop 16/16, food 78 -> 81 (a 5.1-tick
# reserve, 19 produced for 16 eaten), water 25, pollution 0, money 952.
# Re-measured 2026-11-20 (Step083 pollution rule + the W1 allocation law):
# HEALTHY at t=540 and t=660, pop 16/16, food 43 -> 40 (prod 19, eaten 16,
# spoiled 2, net +1/tick), water 80, pollution 0, money 617 -> 857, 0 rejected
# command. The money differs because the park is no longer bought here; the
# refused step used to lay its 5$ approach road anyway (measured 612 -> 852).
# Re-measured 2026-11-21 (wood chain opened, D7 draft): this line now builds a
# lumber camp beside the (4,4) deposit and two parks that tend it — 2 wood/tick
# out, 2/tick regrown, the deposit stays at 100/100, so the workshops never
# starve again and planks stop being a dead end. Measured t=660: HEALTHY, pop
# 16/16, money 619 (net 0/tick), planks 358, 37 buildings (20 operational: the
# camp and the two parks pay 3/tick of upkeep). The chain costs money on this
# short arc; what it buys is planks, which is what housing is built from since
# D3. The age-3 line is where it pays for itself.
